      <p style="margin-bottom:20px;">The combination of Decatur's humid climate and its aging housing stock creates persistent mold risk. Homes in the Old Decatur and Kingsway historic districts — many built before 1950 — have crawlspaces and foundation characteristics that predate modern moisture management standards. High clay soil content, limited crawlspace ventilation, and decades of accumulated moisture intrusion create conditions we find mold in regularly. If your historic Decatur home has never had a professional mold inspection, the probability of finding something is higher than you'd expect.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom:20px;">Decatur's industrial environment also contributes to indoor air quality challenges that aren't purely weather-driven. Particulate matter from the industrial corridor, combined with high pollen loads during spring and fall, creates elevated allergen concentrations in homes throughout the city. Professional carpet and upholstery cleaning in Decatur is as much about air quality as appearance — particularly for families with children or respiratory conditions.</p>
      <p>Winter freeze events in Decatur track with the broader North Alabama pattern — infrequent but consequential. The 2021 freeze event affected Decatur similarly to the rest of the region, with burst pipe damage in homes across the city. We responded to multiple Decatur calls during that event.</p>
